  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 218 PM MDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nStarting on Tuesday, the upper-level ridge pattern starts to \nstall southward and becomes elongated over the majority of \nthe northern half of the United States as the low pressure moves \neastward over southern Canada. It then reamplifies again over \nthe South-Central Plains on Friday. This will allow a weak to \nmoderate strength cold front to pass through the area Tuesday \nevening, small chances for showers throughout the rest of the \nperiod, and keeping temperatures above average. There is medium \nto high confidence (55-70%) that this cold front will cause \nstorms to be minimally severe. Storms are expected to be fast-\nmoving towards the east-northeast with upper-level shear values \nbeing 40 kts. Instability and moisture seems to be the limiting \nfactor as Mixed-Layer CAPE (MLCAPE) will be around 775 J/kg as \nwell as a large surface dew point depression of 35F degrees \ncould be present and a strong, elevated dry layer above 6 km \nhigh. Primary hazards look to be moderate-to-strong winds and a \nslight chance for hail under an inch. \n\nThroughout the remainder of the week, confidence for \nafternoon-to-evening showers starts to  progressively decrease \nto 10% as diurnal and weak embedded shortwave troughs will be \nthe main driving forces for showers. Probability of Precipitation \n(PoPs) has 15% or less for showers over the majority of the \nforecast area. \n\nEven with the chances for storms and showers, temperatures will\nremain above average, but will decrease over time. There is \nmedium confidence (50%) that temperatures Tuesday will be in the\nupper 90s due to the cold front moving through. The NBM 75th to\n25th percentile range spans 8 F degrees and at the time of the \nthat could drop temperatures in the low to mid 90s. Wednesday, \ntemperatures will be in the mid 90s whereas Thursday and Friday \nwill likely drop into the low 90s.",
